--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/exact_first_step_route_reuse.md
@@ -0,0 +1,101 @@
+# Exact first-step route reuse
+
+The SM120 MiniMax-H3 path can derive its sparse mixed-precision route from the
+actual dense INT8 QK computation at the first denoising step and reuse that
+route at later steps. This mode removes the Mean/MeanMax DraftMap from both
+the capture and reuse paths.
+
+## Public attention API
+
+Create one request-local cache and use the same instance for every layer and
+denoising step:
+
+```python
+from anemoi import (
+    AttentionRouteCache,
+    NVFP4Calibration,
+    QuantConfig,
+    SparseConfig,
+    VisualLayout,
+    anemoi_attention,
+)
+
+route_cache = AttentionRouteCache()
+sparse = SparseConfig(query_block_size=64, sparsity_ratio=0.75)
+quant = QuantConfig(nvfp4_ratio=0.60, int8_ratio=0.40)
+
+# Step 0: dense INT8 output plus exact QK-tile importance capture.
+out0 = anemoi_attention(
+    q0, k0, v0,
+    layout=layout,
+    layer=layer,
+    sparse_config=sparse,
+    quant_config=quant,
+    calibration=calibration,
+    first_step=True,
+    route_cache=route_cache,
+)
+
+# Steps 1..N: no DraftMap pooling, scoring, or route sorting.
+out1 = anemoi_attention(
+    q1, k1, v1,
+    layout=layout,
+    layer=layer,
+    sparse_config=sparse,
+    quant_config=quant,
+    calibration=calibration,
+    first_step=False,
+    route_cache=route_cache,
+)
+```
+
+`first_step=True` computes the returned attention output entirely with the
+dense INT8 path. Inside the same QK kernel it reduces each actual quantized
+Q-by-64 physical QK tile to its maximum score. Q128 routing then max-pools each
+adjacent pair of physical K64 tiles into its logical Q128-by-K128 score. The
+existing global fixed-budget router assigns retained tiles to the requested
+precision fractions. The route is stored per layer as a ragged int16 ID stream
+plus row offsets.
+
+`first_step=False` requires a matching cached route. Geometry, device,
+sparsity, precision fractions, and anchor policy are included in the cache
+signature; a mismatch fails instead of silently using a stale route.
+
+The cache is request-local. Clear it before a new request, or let the H3
+adapter clear it when the denoising step sequence restarts.
+
+## MiniMax-H3 adapter
+
+Set `reuse_first_step_route=True` in `H3MPAConfig`. The adapter passes
+`first_step=(step == 0)`, owns the request-local cache, and exposes cached
+entry/byte counts in its runtime statistics.
+
+This mode intentionally bypasses `dense_first_steps`: the first step is dense
+INT8 by construction. `dense_first_layers` remains an independent policy.
+
+## Memory representation
+
+The route cache does not retain an `[heads, query_blocks, key_blocks]` int32
+matrix. Only active tile IDs are stored, ordered by NVFP4, middle precision,
+and FP16 phase, with one offset per route row. The native materializer expands
+one layer's route directly into the physical K64 execution list when needed.
+
+For the measured 75% skip policy on 56-head H3 inputs:
+
+| Q tile | Tokens | Cached route / layer | Approx. 50-layer cache |
+|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| 64 | 38,661 | 10.34 MB | 0.52 GB |
+| 128 | 38,661 | 2.72 MB | 0.14 GB |
+| 64 | 110,013 | 83.60 MB | 4.18 GB |
+
+## Current limitations
+
+- The captured statistic is the maximum actual quantized QK score in each
+  tile. Other reductions (softmax mass, top-k sum, or temporal aggregation)
+  are not yet exposed.
+- The route is fixed after step 0. It does not currently refresh when the
+  attention structure drifts later in denoising.
+- Capture and routing add work to the already-dense first step. The approach
+  is most attractive when the first step is dense for quality anyway, or for
+  long sequences where eliminating repeated quadratic DraftMap routing is
+  valuable.
